PART 2: UNDERSTANDING ONE-WAY DATA FLOW

================================================================================

What is One-Way Data Flow?

One-way data flow means that application data flows downward from parent components to child components.

Concept               | Meaning
----------------------|----------------------------------------------
Direction             | Parent → Child
Data                  | Passed through props
State Ownership       | State belongs to the component that owns it
Props                 | Read-only input for receiving component
State Update          | Performed by the component that owns state
Child Interaction     | Child can call a callback passed by parent
Updated Data          | Flows back down through new props

Visual Representation
PARENT COMPONENT
(owns data/state)
       ↓
       ↓ Props
       ↓
CHILD COMPONENT
(receives data)
       ↓
DISPLAYS DATA
       ↓
USER SEES UI


================================================================================

ONE-WAY DATA FLOW WITH USER INTERACTION

================================================================================

A child cannot directly modify its parent's state.

Instead, the parent can provide a function.

PARENT STATE
     ↓
DATA PASSED AS PROPS
     ↓
CHILD DISPLAYS DATA
     ↓
USER INTERACTS
     ↓
CHILD CALLS CALLBACK PROP
     ↓
PARENT CALLBACK RUNS
     ↓
PARENT UPDATES STATE
     ↓
NEW PROPS FLOW DOWN
     ↓
CHILD RE-RENDERS

Important Distinction
Data                | Direction
--------------------|---------------------------------------
State/values        | Parent → Child through props
Event notification  | Child calls function provided by parent
State update        | Parent performs the update
New state value     | Parent → Child again through props


So React remains one-way even when a child triggers a parent's state update.

================================================================================

ONE-WAY vs DIRECT TWO-WAY MUTATION

================================================================================

One-Way React Pattern           | Direct Two-Way Mutation
--------------------------------|-------------------------------------
Parent owns state               | Multiple components mutate data
Props flow downward             | Data can change from many directions
Child uses callback             | Child directly modifies parent data
Clear update path               | Harder to identify update source
Predictable ownership           | Ownership becomes unclear
Easier to debug                 | Harder to trace


Recommended pattern:

Parent
  ↓
Props
  ↓
Child
  ↓
Callback
  ↓
Parent updates
  ↓
New Props


