PART 3: HOW PROPS ENABLE ONE-WAY DATA FLOW

================================================================================

Props as the Data Channel

Parent:

function Parent() {

  const data =
    'Hello from Parent';

  return (
    <Child message={data} />
  );
}


Child:

function Child(props) {

  return (
    <h1>
      {props.message}
    </h1>
  );
}

What Happens?
Step | Action
-----|----------------------------------------------------------
1    | Parent owns/contains the data
2    | Parent passes data to Child through props
3    | Child receives props
4    | Child reads props.message
5    | Child renders the value
6    | Child does not modify the received prop

Flow
Parent

data = "Hello from Parent"
        ↓
message={data}
        ↓
Child
        ↓
props.message
        ↓
Hello from Parent


================================================================================

PROPS ARE READ-ONLY

================================================================================

A receiving component should treat props as read-only.

Wrong
function Child(props) {

  props.message = 'New value';

  return (
    <h1>
      {props.message}
    </h1>
  );
}

Correct
function Child(props) {

  return (
    <h1>
      {props.message}
    </h1>
  );
}

Why?
Read-Only Props Help With
--------------------------------
Predictable data flow
Clear state ownership
Easier debugging
Reduced accidental mutation
Maintainable component design


Easy memory:

PROPS
  ↓
RECEIVE
  ↓
READ
  ↓
DON'T MUTATE


