PART 8: BEST PRACTICES

================================================================================

Best Practice 1: Keep State at the Highest Needed Level

If multiple sibling components need the same data, place the state in their closest common parent.

Good
function Parent() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0);

  return (
    <>

      <Display count={count} />

      <Button
        onClick={() =>
          setCount(count + 1)
        }
      />

    </>
  );
}


Both children work from the same source of truth.

Avoid Separate Unsynchronized State
function Parent() {

  return (
    <>
      <Display />
      <Button />
    </>
  );
}

function Display() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0);

  return (
    <h1>{count}</h1>
  );
}

function Button() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0);

  return (
    <button
      onClick={() =>
        setCount(count + 1)
      }
    >
      +
    </button>
  );
}


Here Display and Button own separate count states.

Better Principle
SHARED DATA
    ↓
CLOSEST COMMON PARENT
    ↓
PASS DOWN THROUGH PROPS


This pattern is commonly called lifting state up.

================================================================================

Best Practice 2: Pass Intent-Specific Functions

================================================================================

Preferred:

const handleIncrement = () => {
  setCount(count + 1);
};

<Button
  onClick={handleIncrement}
/>


Less encapsulated:

<Button
  onClick={setCount}
/>

Why?
Intent-Specific Callback       | Raw State Setter
-------------------------------|-----------------------------
Describes intended operation   | Exposes state mechanism
Parent controls update logic   | Child may need setter details
Easier to modify later         | More implementation coupling


================================================================================

Best Practice 3: Use Descriptive Prop Names

================================================================================

Good:

<Button
  onClick={handleDelete}
  label="Delete"
/>

<Form
  onSubmit={handleFormSubmit}
/>


Avoid:

<Button
  onClick={fn}
  label="btn"
/>

<Form
  onSubmit={f}
/>

Generic Name  | Better Name
--------------|-----------------
fn            | handleDelete
f             | handleFormSubmit
btn           | Delete


================================================================================

Best Practice 4: Document Important Props

================================================================================

Example:

/**
 * Button Component
 *
 * @param {function} onClick
 *   Function called when button is clicked
 *
 * @param {string} label
 *   Text displayed on the button
 */
function Button({
  onClick,
  label
}) {

  return (
    <button onClick={onClick}>
      {label}
    </button>
  );
}


For larger JavaScript codebases, documentation or type systems such as TypeScript can make component contracts easier to understand and maintain.

