================================================================================

Question                        | Short Answer
--------------------------------|------------------------------------------
What is one-way data flow?      | Data flows from parent to child
How is data passed to child?    | Through props
Can child modify props?         | No, props are read-only
Who should update state?        | Component that owns the state
How can child request update?   | Call a callback passed by parent
What happens after state update?| Parent renders with updated data
What is props drilling?         | Passing props through intermediate levels
How can deep sharing be handled?| Context is one possible option
What is lifting state up?       | Move shared state to common ancestor
Why is one-way flow useful?     | Clear ownership and traceable updates


================================================================================

ONE-LINE INTERVIEW ANSWER

================================================================================

React follows one-way data flow: a parent owns state and passes data down through props. Props are read-only, so when a child needs a change, it calls a callback supplied by the parent; the parent updates its state, and the new value flows back down as updated props.

